you give appropriate credit and license your adaptations under the same terms.
The code carries no such share-alike obligation — MIT lets you do essentially
anything provided the copyright notice travels with it.

## Why two licences

This repository is roughly 80 files of prose and 8 shell scripts. Licensing it
entirely under MIT would be the simpler choice, and it is what most homelab
repositories do — but MIT is written about software, and applying it to a
written guide leaves attribution and derivative works unclear. CC BY-SA is
written for exactly this kind of material. The cost of being accurate is this
one extra file. See `docs/decisions/ADR-021-repository-publication.md`.
